ation of  the environment in you know in  Chesapeake is my primary example that I  run through in the book you know deals  have been done money's been made impacts  have been had but the environment  doesn't seem like it's getting much out  of the deal and our thesis is that the  environment certainly can  and it can get you can do it in a very  efficient way and markets can be enabled  but markets are like rockets they they  need to be I mean Rockets can take you  to unbelievable places places you've  never imagined going but they have to be  built right very soundly and they have  to be targeted right you know if you  have one that's not put together well  and you got it you know leaning on its  side when you blast off it's going to  tear across the landscape and create a  lot of bad things markets are the same  way and so I talk a lot about how to  properly design a market and what those  elements are in the book some of those i  just mentioned here but the  standardization piece is the big piece  and we we can we can do this in a way  that really does make a big difference  environmentally while also generating a  lot of capital flow toward the problems  that we want fixed like the dead zone  like getting colorado to reach the sea  like recovering the fish out here like  fixing the Chesapeake we can do these  things we've got to set it up right and  then get going on it and with that thank  you Joe that concludes the talk on the  new book quantified once again we would  like to thank joe whitworth for joining  us today if you wish to review a  recording of this webinar please visit  the yale center for business and the  environment website or access the  recording through the yale iTunes  University podcast thank you for joining  us until next time this is andrew woo  from the yale center for business and  environment in collaboration with lee  Welton the conservation finance network
 

